[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: A person is on a ladder installing lights on a house.
AUDIO: 'So, last year I had the privilege of meeting Jacob, who\'s one of the best permanent outdoor lighting installers in the country, as well as the creator of Permatrack. And I was able to see firsthand how easy it actually is to do a custom install, so that everything is pretty much invisible during the day, and obviously awesome at night. And as'

[00:13] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Hands demonstrate connecting two pieces of the PermTrack system with a small connector.
AUDIO: 'far as price, if you\'re willing to brave a ladder and have a free Saturday, this can all be done for a little over a thousand dollars, including the lights and tracks, compared to the seven thousand dollars a professional company quoted me. Now, in today\'s video, I\'m super excited because version two of the'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Permatrack is officially available. And for anyone wanting a professional look without the professional price, or if you\'re an installer looking to start a business, this is going to make your install significantly easier and faster. So, let\'s first quickly talk about'

[00:39] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A person holds a single piece of the PermTrack.
AUDIO: 'version one. And what most people don\'t realize is that the professional-grade UV-treated rigid PVC that Jacob uses comes off the manufacturing line looking like this. Then, him and his team had to manually cut this little notch out of every single track that went out the'

[00:52] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A hand demonstrates how two PermTrack pieces connect on a wooden surface.
AUDIO: 'door, as well as manually attaching the sticky pad. And this might not seem like a lot, but with close to half a million of these tracks sold, that represents a significant amount of time. Then, for installation, if you have wood or HardieBoard type soffits, a brad nail'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gun was the quickest. But, if you\'re like me that has vinyl or'

[01:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'aluminum soffits, you would screw in one track, take another piece, hold it in place with one hand, and then screw that in. And even though this process is pretty straightforward and not difficult, there is absolutely room for improvement. Enter version two, and the'

[01:31] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Hands demonstrate inserting a light strip into a PermTrack section.
AUDIO: 'star of the show is this brand new connector clip that they designed. The first major problem it solved is that they no longer have to manually cut out the notch or add the sticky pad, resulting in a much faster production time for them at the warehouse. But, the main benefit for DIY homeowners'

[01:55] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A person on a ladder continues installing PermTrack sections.
AUDIO: 'or installers using the perm track is that now you\'ll secure the first section like I\'m doing here, use the connector piece to attach another section, and now you\'re able to completely let go, which frees up both hands to use for grabbing your tools. And another benefit, because'

[02:08] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Hands demonstrate assembling corner pieces for the PermTrack system.
AUDIO: 'of the rigid PVC and how the tracks are now physically connected, I\'m able to [snorts] get away with only using one screw per section instead of the two before. And I\'ve talked to installers who\'ve used this new system, and they\'ve said on a full house it\'s saving them'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about 2 hours of time on the install. Now moving on, version two also has peak mount covers for the two most common angles you\'ll find on a house. If you go back to version one, you had to line up two tracks and use one of the regular'

[02:33] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Hands snap together two PermTrack corner pieces.
AUDIO: 'covers to position the light at the very top. It did work, but it was sometimes tricky to get the mount cover to stay put, and often times required some glue to hold in place. With these new covers, everything just snaps into place, it\'s perfectly aligned and positioned, and'

[02:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re ready to move on. And of course, version two is still compatible with all the other angle connectors and pieces that you might need for your home. And of course, you don\'t have to paint'

[03:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'these to match your soffits, but my guess is 99% of DIYers will do this step. And if you\'re an installer, it\'s absolutely something that the homeowners will expect. This is extremely easy to do after it\'s on your house, or you may opt to do it beforehand on the ground.'

[03:14] [scene:product_comparison]
VISUAL: A retail display showing a "D-LINE" cable raceway kit for $28.99.
AUDIO: 'But the PVC doesn\'t need any primer, and it\'s paint ready out of the box. So moving on, if there\'s anyone out there that thinks they can save a ton of money by coming up with the track system yourself, I went to my local Menards and found a cable raceway kit used for'

[03:27] [scene:product_comparison]
VISUAL: Hands demonstrate the flexibility of a "Menards track" compared to PermTrack.
AUDIO: 'hiding wires. And for $29, you get 10 ft of track. If you\'re doing the math for a basic 200-ft install, you would need 20 of these, which comes to $580. And guys, that obviously doesn\'t include the puck covers, it\'s not UV protected, it\'s'

[03:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'definitely not rigid PVC, and you\'d still need to cut every section down to the right size. Now as far as metal versus PVC for permanent outdoor lights, PVC is absolutely the way to go. I\'ll be doing'

